now the government has insisted  throughout the coronavirus crisis that  it's been guided by the science we've  seen the experts standing alongside the  prime minister at those daily press  conferences of course - and some are  true report was the government's chief  scientific adviser for four and a half  years he joins us now from West London  thank you very much for being on the  program this morning now I know that  you've been really following and  involved in the development of that  vaccine for covert 19 and when  realistically do you think a vaccine  could be ready well I'd like to start by  picking up the point that Tony Blair has  just made which in fact is that there is  enormous international scientific Co  working on this and so SETI which is the  Coalition for epidemic preparedness  innovation which the UK has just  committed 210 million pounds to has been  working across the world on vaccine  development they have eight in their  active pipeline at the moment one of  which is one that UK research and  innovation and others have been funding  in Oxford and so there is an enormous  amount going on and he's absolutely  right also that science and technology  and innovation is critical to both  providing the advice and the management  as I told you when I bit recently the  reality is that we need to be sure that  a vaccine is effective and doesn't have  side effects or serious side effects so  it will take probably a year to 18  months everyone will work extremely hard  to make it as quick as they possibly can  the regulators are working much faster  than they do normally and so everyone  will do absolutely everything they can  to get it to people as quickly as  possible  and indeed the scale-up of the that  particular oxford vaccine although you  know whether it stands a better chance  than any of the others who knows will  will bear to manufacture up to a million  doses fairly quickly but it's a long way  from protecting the whole population and  as I say the the the other thing we need  to understand is more about the  protective immune response to  coronavirus Tony Blair was also saying  that he thinks the most likely way out  of the lockdown if you like is actually  through mass testing nearly everyone he  said could be tested  and then to find out more as you say  about that immune response and do you  think that that perhaps is the most  realistic way out of the situation we're  in now  well the science advice is actually  absolutely rock-solid worldwide that the  best way out of this is to keep people  apart from each other which is why the  advice of stay at home is so critically  important in other words one thing we  know is that you need to reduce the  number of people that one infected  person affects on in fact on average  which is about 3/4 as far as we think  down to below one and the only way you  do that is by separating people now it  is also absolutely the case that we need  to be able to test people who are  working in the frontline for whether  they have virus infection and if we're  really going to understand and be able  to model the academic properly we need  to know what fraction of the population  has been infected and so really there  are two types of tests there's the test  for the virus and then there's the tests  for whether people have been infected  and have as has been said already on the  program what should be a protective  immune response for at least once if not  a small number of years just to pick up  on that point and an Oxford University  study this week suggested that up to  half of us could actually have been  infected with corona virus do you think  that's at all realistic that paper has  been first it hasn't actually been  peer-reviewed by other scientists and  secondly it has been pretty uniformly  criticized for it the assumptions that's  make in the modeling firstly it assumed  that there were a limited number of  vulnerable people who would succumb and  the evidence is that although there are  the most vulnerable we know we also know  that some people died of this for  reasons we don't yet fully understand in  terms of their susceptibility and  secondly the model basically put in the  fact that there may be large numbers of  uninfected people the evidence such as  we have it emerging from it the example  is that unfortunately there probably  aren't an enormous number of people  who've been infected but completely  asymptomatic okay and now you were  saying there that the evidence shows  that social distancing keeping apart  from each other is the  your most important thing that we can do  but of course that pays the you know  heavy price you pay for that keeping  away from my loved ones it's a difficult  thing for many people to do and  particularly of course if you're in a  small apartment or something like that  as well and now I just wonder if you  have any stay on how long you think that  the lockdown could go on for because if  that's the most effective thing we can  do and the vaccine that's not gonna be  ready for at least probably a year it  feels as if this could go on for many  months yet yes and that is why the best  advice is coming from the modeling  community and Neil Ferguson himself  who's leading the team at Imperial but  it's also important to mention we're not  dependent on a single team of modelers  there are modelers at the London School  of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine there  are other modelers so they're working  with each other and I can tell you  they're not suffering from groupthink  because these are a very feisty lot of  researchers who you know will argue  right down to them their last details  but what Neil Ferguson is saying is  until at least the end of May and  possibly - but of course that's the  operational challenge because as you say  there's the balance between keeping  society going as normally as it possibly  can making sure that all the supply  lines work minimising that disruption  which of course has potential for  adverse mental health consequences that  we know about from isolation so it's  always that balance between clamping  down on your disease spread whilst at  the same time keeping society going as  normally as we possibly can in an  extraordinary abnormal time and that's  why this real-time modelling this  real-time input into the politicians who  are leading the response by researchers  on the scientific advice group and  emergency reporting - Patra valance and  Chris witty  that's an embedded mechanism that we  have in the UK and I think it is really  among the very best in the world do you  think we can see a situation where we  effectively alternate between periods of  stricter look down relaxing things  slightly and  up a bit more well so I think that is a  possibility and of course everyone is  looking very carefully at a China and  Korea China was obviously sadly ahead of  everyone else in this in terms of the  timing of the epidemic it's the disease  emerged in Wuhan and they've clamped  down very hard and I think everyone  around the world who understands the  epidemiology is looking to see whether  the outbreak does start again because  there's no doubt that cases will have  gone right across the whole of China so  there may be very low levels infection  and again it's that balance between a  stop start stop start and trying to  smooth out the epidemic but having also  I think in the next phase it will be  really important at a time when we have  much better and more abundant testing to  do that testing whenever an outbreak  seems to flare up again and countries  around the world are cooling themselves  up to be able to do that okay thank you  very much for being on the program thank  you very much thank you

